WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.567 --> 00:00:04.100
-We will help you carve out
the most problematic areas

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.067
and what we can do with them
because I think

00:00:06.067 --> 00:00:08.400
a common misconception
around death cleaning

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.634
is that we're just here to like
throw things out and declutter

00:00:11.634 --> 00:00:13.300
and like we're done with it.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.934
But that's not what
it's about. At its heart,

00:00:15.934 --> 00:00:19.434
it's about discovering
what's important to you.

00:00:19.434 --> 00:00:23.501
-Yeah.
-And what are your hopes
for these things?

00:00:23.501 --> 00:00:24.701
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:24.701 --> 00:00:27.234
We're here with Amy Poehler,
everybody.

00:00:27.234 --> 00:00:32.300
That was a clip from "The Gentle
Art of Swedish Death Cleaning."

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:34.634
"The Gentle Art of Swedish
Death Cleaning."

00:00:34.634 --> 00:00:37.467
Not a show when I met you
almost two decades ago

00:00:37.467 --> 00:00:39.467
I would have thought
would be a show you'd produce.

00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:42.601
-Yeah, well, I'm always
growing and changing and...

00:00:42.601 --> 00:00:44.067
But it's so cool.

00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:48.601
It's based off of a book,
and it is this idea of kind of

00:00:48.601 --> 00:00:51.334
kind of how do you want to go
through your things?

00:00:51.334 --> 00:00:52.434
How do you want
to live your life?

00:00:52.434 --> 00:00:53.667
What conversations
do you want to have

00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:54.667
while you're still around

00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:56.601
and what stuff
can you get rid of

00:00:56.601 --> 00:00:59.968
to not burden the people
who are left when you're gone?

00:00:59.968 --> 00:01:04.868
So I do the voice-over for it.
We produced it along with Scout,

00:01:04.868 --> 00:01:06.501
the great producers
who do "Queer Eye."

00:01:06.501 --> 00:01:09.534
We have three great
Swedish death cleaners

00:01:09.534 --> 00:01:12.601
who come in
and are like incredibly

00:01:12.601 --> 00:01:15.300
Swedish in the best --
in the best way.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:17.033
I mean that because
they're so direct.

00:01:17.033 --> 00:01:19.801
-Yes.
-And I think clarity is
kindness.

00:01:19.801 --> 00:01:21.667
I like direct people.
-Yep.

00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:23.467
-As you can probably tell.

00:01:23.467 --> 00:01:27.534
And so they just say very --
They get to the point.

00:01:27.534 --> 00:01:29.801
-They're almost the opposite
of magicians.

00:01:29.801 --> 00:01:32.901
-Uh, 100%.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:32.901 --> 00:01:35.567
Magicians are like,
"I'm over here, I'm over there."

00:01:35.567 --> 00:01:38.200
And you're like,
"No, you're going to jail.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:41.267
You're in jail. That's where
you should be -- in jail."

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:45.501
-So your brother, married
to a wonderful Swedish woman,

00:01:45.501 --> 00:01:47.200
has a Swedish family,
lives in Sweden.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.868
So you've become very --
you've become very aware

00:01:49.868 --> 00:01:52.167
of the beautiful benefits
of the Swedes.

00:01:52.167 --> 00:01:53.734
-And you would think
I could speak more Swedish,

00:01:53.734 --> 00:01:55.701
but I can't.
But I love that country.

00:01:55.701 --> 00:01:58.501
And my brother lives there
with his family and yeah,

00:01:58.501 --> 00:02:00.634
and the Swedes have this way of
just saying like,

00:02:00.634 --> 00:02:02.067
"This is ugly."

00:02:02.067 --> 00:02:04.000
[ Laughter ]

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.100
"You don't like it and we don't
like it

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.901
and you need to go --
it needs to go away."

00:02:07.901 --> 00:02:09.834
And you're just like, "Wow."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:09.834 --> 00:02:12.767
They say it really fast
and I like that about them.

00:02:12.767 --> 00:02:15.400
-And do you find having, like,
engaged with this,

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.734
do you find you're good
at that now in your life?

00:02:17.734 --> 00:02:18.834
Are you getting rid of things?

00:02:18.834 --> 00:02:20.934
Are there things
that you're keeping

00:02:20.934 --> 00:02:24.567
because you want to be able
to pass them on?

00:02:24.567 --> 00:02:26.534
-Yeah, I think, right?
Everybody's trying to figure out

00:02:26.534 --> 00:02:27.901
what they should keep
for their kids,

00:02:27.901 --> 00:02:29.968
what their kids will like,
especially we have kids,

00:02:29.968 --> 00:02:33.501
and you think about what their
first apartment,

00:02:33.501 --> 00:02:35.934
if they would like
this old rotary phone.

00:02:35.934 --> 00:02:38.367
[ Laughter ]

00:02:38.367 --> 00:02:41.834
You're like, "How many DVDs
are they going to be into?"

00:02:41.834 --> 00:02:43.100
-They might come back.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:44.567
I feel as though by the time
your kids go to college.

00:02:44.567 --> 00:02:46.234
-Well, not --

00:02:46.234 --> 00:02:50.000
I do feel, though,
that A.I. is going to kill us.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:51.567
-Uh-huh.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:51.567 --> 00:02:53.133
-And I, you know,
we're Gen X,

00:02:53.133 --> 00:02:55.901
so I think we are
the last generation

00:02:55.901 --> 00:02:58.133
that are not going
to mate with robots.

00:02:58.133 --> 00:03:00.133
[ Laughter ]

00:03:00.133 --> 00:03:03.334
I think we will
fight against the robots.

00:03:03.334 --> 00:03:06.167
But Gen Z will love
and marry robots.

00:03:06.167 --> 00:03:07.467
-Right.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:07.467 --> 00:03:09.701
And that will be hard
because we will be obviously

00:03:09.701 --> 00:03:12.501
waging some sort of sort
of underground revolution

00:03:12.501 --> 00:03:14.367
against the robots
and at the same time,

00:03:14.367 --> 00:03:15.801
your sons are going
to bring them home.

00:03:15.801 --> 00:03:17.667
-Exactly, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:17.667 --> 00:03:19.267
-And then you're
going to have to act

00:03:19.267 --> 00:03:20.734
like you like the robot.

00:03:20.734 --> 00:03:22.501
-Exactly. You're going to
have to make the choice.

00:03:22.501 --> 00:03:26.901
Like, do I save
my half robot, half human son?

00:03:26.901 --> 00:03:29.167
-Yeah.
-Or do I kill him because I know

00:03:29.167 --> 00:03:30.767
he's going to put
worms in my brain

00:03:30.767 --> 00:03:32.234
or whatever they're going to do?

00:03:32.234 --> 00:03:33.968
I can't even imagine
what -- I'm no robot.

00:03:33.968 --> 00:03:35.567
I'm a human.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:35.567 --> 00:03:37.734
But I don't like it.
and I won't date a robot.

00:03:37.734 --> 00:03:38.834
-You won't?
-No.

00:03:38.834 --> 00:03:40.234
-Interesting. Even if --

00:03:40.234 --> 00:03:42.501
-Nope. No matter how hard
you try.

00:03:42.501 --> 00:03:44.167
-Can I try to sell you on it?
Alright.

00:03:44.167 --> 00:03:45.934
-You're not going to sell me
on it. I'm not dating a robot.

00:03:45.934 --> 00:03:47.367
-Okay, but here's the thing.

00:03:47.367 --> 00:03:50.634
Here's how the A.I. love robot
that we're developing works.

00:03:50.634 --> 00:03:52.467
At first it's going
to be bad, right?

00:03:52.467 --> 00:03:54.801
It's not going to work out.
But each successive date,

00:03:54.801 --> 00:03:57.033
the A.I. will learn
more about you.

00:03:57.033 --> 00:03:59.801
And then -- so by like date 10,
you'll be like,

00:03:59.801 --> 00:04:02.601
"Oh, we really understand."
You have to feed the A.I. robot.

00:04:02.601 --> 00:04:04.267
-Oh, I have to feed it?

00:04:04.267 --> 00:04:05.434
[ Laughter ]

00:04:05.434 --> 00:04:08.300
Oh, great. So it's like
a human man, then?

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:09.467
Is that what you're
talking about?

00:04:09.467 --> 00:04:13.000
[ Laughter ]
I have to tell it what I like

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.367
and then it knows?
-Yeah.

00:04:15.367 --> 00:04:17.834
Also, you got to plug it in. You
got to remember to plug it in.

00:04:17.834 --> 00:04:20.601
-I have to return every couple
of years --

00:04:20.601 --> 00:04:22.133
I'm like, "It's broken.
I need a new --"

00:04:22.133 --> 00:04:23.234
It's like the iPhone.

00:04:23.234 --> 00:04:25.300
I'm like, "I need
a new robot boyfriend."

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:28.501
No, I won't date a robot
no matter how hard you try.

00:04:28.501 --> 00:04:30.968
Unless...
-Okay.

00:04:30.968 --> 00:04:32.634
-He picks up food
on the way home.

00:04:32.634 --> 00:04:35.167
[ Laughter ]
-Wait. That's the one thing?

00:04:35.167 --> 00:04:37.534
I feel like robots
almost already do that.

00:04:37.534 --> 00:04:39.701
-Yeah. Oh, yeah.
Those robots that, like, go --

00:04:39.701 --> 00:04:40.868
Have you seen them
on the street?

00:04:40.868 --> 00:04:42.601
Well, you guys probably
don't care

00:04:42.601 --> 00:04:44.200
because you're all Gen Z,
and you're like,

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:45.667
"That's cute."
-Yeah.

00:04:45.667 --> 00:04:48.767
-It's not going to be cute
when you go home

00:04:48.767 --> 00:04:51.067
and you lift up your covers
and it's living there

00:04:51.067 --> 00:04:53.767
and it's going to kill you
and strangle you.

00:04:53.767 --> 00:04:57.067
-Do you feel like --
Are you overwhelmed

00:04:57.067 --> 00:04:59.601
by the technology
your kids are engaging with?

00:04:59.601 --> 00:05:02.200
-Yeah. Although, you know,
have you tried that chat --

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:05.501
Maybe you guys have done bits
on it here about ChatGPT.

00:05:05.501 --> 00:05:08.067
-Yeah, no, we haven't really
messed around with it much.

00:05:08.067 --> 00:05:09.300
-You haven't messed
around with it?
-No.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:12.033
-Okay. So it can't
really write a joke yet.

00:05:12.033 --> 00:05:16.167
I don't think. And once it
writes a joke, like game's over.

00:05:16.167 --> 00:05:17.734
-Yeah. No, that's actually --
-That's it.

00:05:17.734 --> 00:05:20.133
-We're just going to do
one real sad last show.

00:05:20.133 --> 00:05:21.934
-Yeah.

00:05:21.934 --> 00:05:23.534
-There's going to be
a robot dressed

00:05:23.534 --> 00:05:25.133
just like me
sitting there waiting.

00:05:25.133 --> 00:05:26.934
-Yeah.
-Like [Whirring]

00:05:26.934 --> 00:05:31.133
-And then it's just like, yeah,
it can't write comedy yet,

00:05:31.133 --> 00:05:33.234
but, I mean, I'm sure
it will in like a week.

00:05:33.234 --> 00:05:35.367
-Yeah. This is like --
You know what?

00:05:35.367 --> 00:05:37.300
It's so great that you're here
on our last week.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:38.968
[ Laughter ]

00:05:38.968 --> 00:05:41.534
-Our last human week.
-Oh, man.

00:05:41.534 --> 00:05:43.868
What a great thing to have you
here on our last human week.

00:05:43.868 --> 00:05:45.501
You were here
on our first human week,

00:05:45.501 --> 00:05:46.734
and you were here on our last.

00:05:46.734 --> 00:05:47.901
-I know you're --

00:05:47.901 --> 00:05:50.067
When is your anniversary
for your 10 years?

00:05:50.067 --> 00:05:51.400
Next year?
-Next February is 10 years.

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:52.667
-Wow. That's awesome.
-Isn't that crazy?

00:05:52.667 --> 00:05:54.434
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:54.434 --> 00:05:55.701
And I always say it.

00:05:55.701 --> 00:05:57.400
I mean, I got to give credit
where credit is due

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:00.367
because our first show,
you were first.

00:06:00.367 --> 00:06:03.100
It was very important to me
that you were our first guest.

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:05.767
And then that meant we
had to ask the second guest,

00:06:05.767 --> 00:06:09.200
who was a young fellow named
Vice President Joe Biden.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:10.400
[ Laughter ]

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:11.767
Now, usually vice presidents,

00:06:11.767 --> 00:06:14.000
when they're on a 12:30 show,
would go first.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:15.634
-Yes.
-And we did say to Joe Biden,

00:06:15.634 --> 00:06:17.167
"Would you be okay following
Amy Poehler?"

00:06:17.167 --> 00:06:19.501
And to his credit, he was like,
"I'll follow her anywhere."

00:06:19.501 --> 00:06:23.300
So very, very charming,
very sweet of him.

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:24.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:24.767 --> 00:06:26.267
Well, it's lovely
to have you here, as always.

00:06:26.267 --> 00:06:30.367
How about break a leg, wherever
you may bring your legs?

00:06:30.367 --> 00:06:32.467
I'm very excited for
the Restless Leg tour.

00:06:32.467 --> 00:06:33.801
I think we're all very excited.

00:06:33.801 --> 00:06:35.834
-Thank you very much, Seth.
I really appreciate it.

00:06:35.834 --> 00:06:37.300
And I love coming here.
You know I love you.

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:38.601
Thank you so much.
I love you. Goodbye.

00:06:38.601 --> 00:06:40.434
-Pick up the check.
Pick up the check.

00:06:40.434 --> 00:06:43.801
Pick up the check.
Amy Poehler, everybody.

